SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ansprache von Bundeskanzler Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-02-24","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-de/service/newsletter-und-abos/bulletin/ansprache-von-bundeskanzler-olaf-scholz-2007936","claim":"Scholz said Russia's attack on Ukraine was an illegal breach of the UN Charter and told Zelenskyy that Ukraine and its elected government had Germany's full solidarity; he also said Russia alone had decided on the war and must stop the fighting immediately.","locator":"lines 195-209","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government address from the first day of the invasion; strong for direct ruler attribution and dated crisis framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate 2022 invasion response.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz statement and choice of framing; not inherited policy.","contrary_evidence":["Self-justifying official speech; does not prove later implementation quality.","Condemnation and solidarity are rhetoric unless matched by policy follow-through."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rede von Bundeskanzler Olaf Scholz","publisher":"Bundesregierung","publication_date":"2022-03-23","url":"https://www.bundesregierung.de/breg-de/service/newsletter-und-abos/bulletin/rede-von-bundeskanzler-olaf-scholz-2019954","claim":"Scholz said Ukraine could rely on German help, that Germany had been supplying defensive weapons since the start of the war, and that NATO would not become a party to the war because a direct confrontation with Russia had to be avoided.","locator":"lines 196-203","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government speech with direct, dated operational commitments and escalation limits.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and central to Germany's Ukraine policy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz conduct as head of government.","contrary_evidence":["The speech also confirms Germany was escalating sanctions and weapons support, which some critics saw as too slow.","Avoiding NATO entry can be read as restraint or as caution that limited support."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.6","2B.7","2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Scholz defends Germany's caution on sending heavy arms to Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-05-01","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/01/uk-ukraine-crisis-germany-scholz","claim":"Scholz said he would rather be cautious than make hasty decisions on heavy weapons for Ukraine, and Reuters reported that Germany was under pressure for initial hesitancy and that allies feared broader war escalation.","locator":"lines 654-670","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting with direct quotes from Scholz and clear criticism context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, during the heavy-weapons debate.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Scholz's choice to prioritize caution and alliance coordination.","contrary_evidence":["This is a restraint rationale, but critics viewed it as indecision and delay.","Later approval of Gepard tanks suggests the initial caution was not the final position."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Germany's Scholz pushes for Ukraine ceasefire in call with Putin","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://uk.investing.com/news/commodities-news/germanys-scholz-pushes-for-ukraine-ceasefire-in-call-with-putin-2648262","claim":"During a call with Putin, Scholz urged an immediate ceasefire, saying a truce was needed to improve the humanitarian situation and make progress toward a diplomatic solution.","locator":"lines 422-425","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a contemporaneous government-spokesperson summary of Scholz's direct call with Putin.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and highly relevant to de-escalation and diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz diplomacy with Russia's leader.","contrary_evidence":["The call did not itself produce a ceasefire.","A ceasefire push can coexist with continued arms support and sanctions."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin will not dictate Ukraine peace terms: Scholz","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/27/us-davos-meeting-scholz","claim":"Scholz said there would be no dictated peace, that Putin must not determine the terms of any settlement, and that continued Western support was needed until Russia accepted it could not win.","locator":"lines 664-671","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters coverage of Scholz's Davos speech; direct quotation of his public peace stance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and central to peace-settlement framing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz position on settlement terms.","contrary_evidence":["Rejecting dictated peace is lawful and pro-sovereignty, but it can also prolong war if no off-ramp exists.","This is rhetorical support for Ukraine, not a negotiated settlement itself."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Statement in support of Ukraine and the proceedings before the International Court of Justice (ICJ)","publisher":"Federal Foreign Office","publication_date":"2022-07-13","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/en/newsroom/news/2542002-2542002","claim":"Germany joined a multistate statement saying Russia had no lawful basis to use military force in Ukraine on the basis of unsubstantiated genocide allegations and urging Russia to suspend its military operations as ordered by the ICJ.","locator":"lines 98-99","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary diplomatic statement tying Germany to legal accountability and a lawful settlement framework.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and highly relevant to lawful security and peace constraints.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Scholz; not a unilateral personal act, but clearly the German government's position.","contrary_evidence":["This is multilateral and legal-process oriented, not evidence of direct Scholz authorship.","It states a legal position rather than demonstrating enforcement power."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin must recognize he cannot win Ukraine war -Scholz at U.N","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-20","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/putin-must-recognize-he-cannot-win-ukraine-war-scholz-at-un-2895908","claim":"At the UN General Assembly, Scholz said Germany would not accept any peace dictated by Russia, argued that Putin's war threatened the global rules-based peace order, and said Ukraine must be able to fend off Russia's attack.","locator":"lines 420-431","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a major contemporaneous speech, with direct quotations and clear context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, late-year strategic framing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Scholz speech and diplomatic line.","contrary_evidence":["Strong anti-aggression rhetoric does not itself end the war.","The speech aligns with allied positions and should not be over-attributed as uniquely German initiative."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erklärungen des Auswärtigen Amts in der Regierungs-pressekonferenz vom 09.12.2022","publisher":"Auswärtiges Amt","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://www.auswaertiges-amt.de/de/newsroom/regierungspressekonferenz-2568802","claim":"The German government said Ukraine is the attacked state, may exercise self-defense under Article 51 of the UN Charter even beyond its own territory against Russian attacks, and that German-supplied weapons are provided for that defensive purpose.","locator":"lines 195-206","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government press briefing; strong for lawful-self-defense framing and end-use policy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to end-of-year conduct and lawful security framing.","ruler_attribution":"Government position under Scholz; the spokesperson is not Scholz personally, but it reflects the government line.","contrary_evidence":["This resolves the legal framing, not the operational risk of escalation or misuse.","The Q&A exposes some ambiguity around how far forward self-defense may extend."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.6","2B.10"]}

**Search And Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents and kept 8 reused items plus 8 new source-claim units as the core chapter set, staying focused on 2022 conduct rather than broad Ukraine background.

Considered but not retained as primary chapter evidence:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications of the same Scholz quotes across Euronews, Investing.com, Japan Times, and other mirrors.
- Out-of-period retrospectives and 2023-2026 summary pages that were useful for orientation but not direct 2022 evidence.
- General NATO nuclear-sharing background pages that were better suited to Chapter 1B than Chapter 2B.
- The 18 February German-French de-escalation statement on Donbas and Ukraine's borders: useful context, but I excluded it from the retained set because the direct actors were the foreign ministers rather than Scholz himself.
- The 2 March, 12 October, and 14 November UN press releases on Ukraine: relevant context, but I kept the more Scholz-linked and German-government-linked legal/diplomatic items instead.
- Reuters-syndicated reports on heavy-weapons hesitation and later peace messaging that repeated the same underlying episode without adding materially new facts.
- One attempted Reuters open produced an internal error, so I did not rely on it.

Broadly, the retained set covers:
- authority and constraints
- invasion-day condemnation and solidarity
- defensive support for Ukraine
- direct ceasefire diplomacy with Putin
- refusal of dictated peace
- legal framing through ICJ and UN channels
- later-year self-defense/end-use clarification
- one critical counterpoint on caution and delay

**Remaining Gaps**
- I still do not have private cabinet minutes, internal NATO notes, or direct evidence of Scholz's personal role in every alliance decision.
- The record on the heavy-weapons debate is clear enough to show hesitation and later movement, but not enough to quantify how much of the delay was Scholz personally versus coalition, military-stock, or alliance coordination constraints.
- I did not find a clean, ruler-specific source showing a formal 2022 peace initiative led by Scholz beyond calls for ceasefire, sanctions pressure, and legal multilateralism.
- Germany was not a belligerent, so 2B.4 and 2B.5 are necessarily about support policy, end-use framing, and legal restraint rather than battlefield conduct; that limits how far the evidence can go.
- If you want more depth, the best next search would be German-language coverage of the 2022 heavy-weapons cabinet disputes and any archival Bundestag/Chancellery material on Scholz's direct Ukraine calls.
